\chapter{Introduction}
\section{Overview}

\textbf{ClimateProcessingEngine} is an open-source Python framework designed for processing large-scale climate datasets, performing statistical distribution fitting, and generating climatological summaries. The engine is optimized for station-wise time series data with support for multiple variables (temperature, precipitation, etc.) and handles missing data through robust window-based approaches.

\subsection{Key Features}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Block-oriented processing} – Handles millions of stations efficiently using configurable block sizes
    \item \textbf{Multi-distribution fitting} – Supports Normal, Skew-Normal, Bimodal, and Pearson Type III distributions
    \item \textbf{Parallel processing} – Optional multi-core execution for accelerated computation
    \item \textbf{Checkpoint recovery} – Automatic resumption from the last completed block
    \item \textbf{Zarr output} – Efficient, chunked, cloud-optimized storage format
    \item \textbf{Comprehensive validation} – Data integrity checks at multiple pipeline stages
    \item \textbf{Benchmarking} – Automatic selection of optimal block size based on system resources
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Target Audience}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Climate scientists and meteorologists
    \item Environmental researchers
    \item Data engineers working with geospatial time series
    \item Graduate students in climate science
\end{itemize}

\subsection{System Requirements}
\begin{table}[H]
    \centering
    \caption{Minimum System Requirements}
    \begin{tabular}{l l}
        \toprule
        \textbf{Component} & \textbf{Requirement} \\
        \midrule
        Operating System & Windows 10/11, Linux, or macOS \\
        Python & 3.8 or higher \\
        RAM & 16 GB minimum (32 GB recommended) \\
        Storage & 100 GB free space (for intermediate and output data) \\
        CPU & 4 cores minimum (8+ recommended for parallel mode) \\
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
\end{table}

