\section{Zarr Schema Module (\texttt{zarr\_schema.py})}
\subsection{Overview}
The \texttt{zarr\_schema.py} module defines the output schema for the Zarr store. It serves as the single source of truth for all output variables.

\subsection{Variable Definitions}
Each output variable is defined as a tuple: \texttt{(name, dtype, fill\_value, description)}.
\begin{table}[H]
    \centering
    \caption{Output Variables per Temperature Variable}
    \begin{tabular}{l l l}
        \toprule
        \textbf{Category} & \textbf{Variables} & \textbf{Count} \\
        \midrule
        Best Distribution & \texttt{best\_dist} & 1 \\
        Statistics & \texttt{mean, std, skewness, median, count} & 5 \\
        Normal Parameters & \texttt{normal\_p1, p2, loglik, aicc, bic} & 5 \\
        Skew-Normal Parameters & \texttt{skew\_p1, p2, p3, loglik, aicc, bic} & 6 \\
        Bimodal Parameters & \texttt{bimodal\_p1, p2, p3, p4, p5, loglik, aicc, bic} & 8 \\
        Pearson III Parameters & \texttt{pearson\_p1, p2, p3, loglik, aicc} & 5 \\
        \midrule
        \textbf{Total per variable} & & \textbf{30} \\
        \textbf{Total for 3 variables} & & \textbf{90} \\
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
\end{table}
\textbf{Note:} The actual implementation uses 30 outputs per variable (excluding \texttt{pearson\_bic}), resulting in 87 total variables.

